# `M198` S3 / `C114` — the known-answer plant for what a locator failure *says* (`M189-02`).
#
# Six of these seven tests are **meant to fail**. That is the whole construction: a locator that
# resolves has a token to write and `locator-near-miss.tflw` reads it, but a locator that resolves
# to nothing has only a sentence, and the sentence is what ten registry mutations move without
# moving any verdict. Every browser plant in this repository runs through the diagnosis formatter
# and reads the element that answered instead, which is `M189a`'s measurement of this family.
#
# So nothing below is graded by its own `ok`. `scripts/verify-construct-acceptance.mjs` reads the
# failure text out of `report/results.json` and asks what it contains — and, for two of them, what
# it must *not* contain, which is the half a "does it fail?" assertion can never reach.
#
# The page is `/diagnose-fixture` (`webV2/src/pages/DiagnoseFixturePage.tsx`), whose header says
# what each section is for.

@constructs @locators @ui @diagnosis
test "a near miss on a button names the real buttons, once each, with the ambiguity of the suggestion"
  # **Meant to fail.** Two `Save draft` buttons and one unnamed icon-only button: the diagnosis
  # must dedupe the pair into one suggestion, say that two elements render it, and still have a
  # slot left for the unnamed control's generated CSS path.
  open "/diagnose-fixture"
  expect button "Save drarft" is visible

@constructs @locators @ui @diagnosis
test "a button that resolved and failed on its state is not answered with other buttons"
  # **Meant to fail**, on the state. `Publish release` is right there and enabled — a nearest-matches
  # list here points away from the cause, which is the zero-match guard's whole job.
  open "/diagnose-fixture"
  expect button "Publish release" is disabled

@constructs @locators @ui @diagnosis
test "an assertion that passes carries no diagnosis"
  # **Meant to pass.** `is hidden` against a name nothing renders is satisfied by the absence, so
  # the element is legitimately not found and the step is a success. A diagnosis appended regardless
  # of outcome staples "nearest matches on the page" to a green step.
  open "/diagnose-fixture"
  expect button "Sprocket calibration" is hidden

@constructs @locators @ui @diagnosis
test "a near miss on text is not answered with the page's structure"
  # **Meant to fail.** `text`'s scan is `*` and a name is computed only for leaves, so every element
  # with children is unnamed — the arm that rescues an icon-only button would answer here with
  # `css "html"` and its neighbours, structural containers in document order, one of which can never
  # be visible.
  open "/diagnose-fixture"
  expect text "Inventroy reconciled" is visible

@constructs @locators @ui @diagnosis
test "wait until carries the same diagnosis when it gives up"
  # **Meant to fail**, by timeout. A suite that only ever wired the diagnosis into `expect`/`check`
  # would never notice this one was missing.
  open "/diagnose-fixture"
  wait until button "Save drarft" is visible

@constructs @locators @ui @diagnosis
test "an ambiguous locator lists what tells the matches apart"
  # **Meant to fail**, on ambiguity. Twelve `Retire` buttons with one distinct label between them:
  # a list of twelve identical quoted strings carries zero bits for the choice it is demanding.
  open "/diagnose-fixture"
  click button "Retire"

@constructs @locators @ui @diagnosis
test "the page is the one the other six ran against"
  # **Meant to pass**, and it is the control: every assertion above is about a sentence, so a page
  # that failed to render would produce six plausible failures and no signal at all.
  open "/diagnose-fixture"
  expect text "Inventory reconciled across every warehouse." is visible
  expect button "Publish release" is visible
